        "learnerExperience": "Learners add digestion to the single class model on chart paper. Using arrows and labels, they show: ugali (starch) in the mouth, saliva amylase (a protein enzyme) acting at about 37 C and near pH 6.8, starch broken into maltose, then into glucose that enters the blood for energy and is stored as glycogen (about 2,000 kcal store for a marathon costing about 2,200 kcal). They add a note box: 'Heat above 60 C or pH 2 or 12 denatures enzymes' and 'Amylase is specific to starch'. They also connect beans and milk (proteins) and groundnuts (lipids) as other foods that need their own enzymes. Each group states one revision to the initial model from Lesson 1 and writes it on a sticky note.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Our model from Lesson 1 showed food going in and energy coming out. What is missing in between?' WAIT TIME of 10-15 seconds. Cold-call 4 learners to add one part with a reason from today's data. Ask: 'Where in our model would Kipchoge's body be at 37 C, and why does that matter?' Guide the class to draw the digestion step between food and energy without re-teaching carbohydrates, proteins or lipids. Close with the celebration: ask learners to tick the feelings 'I feel accomplished', 'I value the process', 'I am confident'. Say: 'You didn't just learn ABOUT science. You DID science!' Preview Lesson 6: 'Next we will use all our evidence to decide which meal best supports the athlete, citing numbers.'",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Cumulative model revision: adding a new component (enzymes and digestion) to a single class model shows how each piece of evidence changes the explanation of the phenomenon. Learners justify each addition with data.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Look for: model includes amylase, starch to maltose to glucose, an optimum label with temperature 37 C and pH 6.8, and a denaturation note. Each addition is justified by a data point. Exit check: ask each learner to write one sentence beginning 'Amylase is affected by temperature because...'. Responses should mention shape changing or denaturing."
